  "landfallEstimate": "Tracking over Bay of Bengal; Doppler radars monitoring",
  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 15\nDEMS-RSMC SPECIAL TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 03.11.2026\nFROM: RSMC \u2013TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTO: STORM WARNING CENTRE, NAYPYITAW (MYANMAR)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, BANGKOK (THAILAND)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, COLOMBO (SRILANKA)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, DHAKA (BANGLADESH)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, KARACHI (PAKISTAN)\nMETEOROLOGICAL OFFICE, MALE (MALDIVES)\nOMAN METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT,\nMUSCAT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nYEMEN METEOROLOGICAL SERVICES,\nREPUBLIC OF YEMEN (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nNATIONAL CENTRE FOR METEOROLOGY, UAE (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nPRESIDENCY OF METEOROLOGY AND ENVIRONMENT,\nSAUDI ARABIA (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nIRAN METEOROLOGICAL ORGANISATION, (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nQATAR METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 15 FOR NORTH INDIAN OCEAN (THE BAY OF\nBENGAL AND ARABIAN SEA) ISSUED AT 0600 UTC OF 03.11.2026 BASED ON 0300 UTC\nOF 03.11.2026\nSub: - Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cMurasu\u201d [Pronunciation: Murasu] over South Coastal Andhra\nPradesh.\nThe Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cMurasu\u201d [Pronunciation: Murasu]\u201d over south coastal Andhra Pradesh moved\nnorth-northwestwards with a speed of 08 kmph during past 06 hours and lay centered at 0300 UTC of\ntoday the 03rd November, 2026 over the same region, near Latitude 15.7\u00b0N and Longitude 80.2\u00b0E about\n30 km north-northeast of Ongole (43221, Andhra Pradesh), 40 km southwest of Bapatla (43220, Andhra\nPradesh), 90 km north-northeast of Kavali (43243, Andhra Pradesh), and 120 km west-southwest of\nMachilipatnam (43185, Andhra Pradesh) and 100 km south-southwest of Vijayawada (43180, Andhra\nPradesh),\nIt is very likely to continue to move further north-northwestwards and weaken gradually into a cyclonic\nstorm (wind speed reaching 80-90 kmph gusting to 100 kmph) by 0600 UTC and further into a deep\ndepression (wind speed reaching 55-65 kmph gusting to 75 kmph) by 1800 UTC of today, the 3rd\nNovember, 2026.\nIt is under the continuous surveillance of Doppler Weather Radars at Machilipatnam and Visakhapatnam.\nCloud distribution: (a) Isolated: <25%, Scattered:25-50%, Broken: 51-75%, Solid:>75%, Convection Intensity: (a) Weak: Cloud Top Temperature(CTT)>-\n25\u00b0C, (b) Moderate: CTT:-25\u00b0Cto-40\u00b0C, (c) Intense: CTT: -41\u00b0Cto -70\u00b0C and (d) Very Intense::Less than -70\u00b0C Probability of cyclogenesis (formation of\ndepression) :NIL:0%, LOW:1-33%, MODERATE:34-66% and HIGH:67-100%\nThis is a guidance Bulletin for WMO/ESCAP Panel Member countries. Visit respective National websites for Country specific Bulletins.\nForecast location and intensity are given in following table:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained surface Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. 0E) wind speed (Kmph) disturbance\n03.11.26/0300 15.7/80.2 95-105 gusting to 115 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n03.11.26/0600 15.9/80.1 80-90 gusting to 100 Cyclonic Storm\n03.11.26/1200 16.3/80.0 65-75 gusting to 85 Cyclonic Storm\n03.11.26/1800 16.7/80.0 40-50 gusting to 60 Depression\n04.11.26/0000 17.2/80.0 25-35 gusting to 45 Well Marked Low Pressure Area\nThe estimated central pressure is 996.0 hPa and maximum sustained wind speed is 55 kt\ngusting to 65kt.\nNellore (43245, Andhra Pradesh) reported mean sea level (MSLP) of 1013.1 hPa, 24 hours pressure\nchange (P24) as 1.6 hPa and maximum sustained wind speed (MSW) as 2000/03kt, Kavali (43243,\nAndhra Pradesh) reported MSLP of 1012.6 hPa, P24 as 0.1 hPa and MSW 2500/02kt, Bapatla\n(43220, Andhra Pradesh) reported MSLP of 1007.9 hPa, P24 as -5.2 hPa and MSW 1100/13kt,\nOngole (43221, Andhra Pradesh) reported MSLP of 1005.4 hPa, P24 as -7.0 hPa and MSW\n2300/19kt.\nAs per latest INSAT 3DS imagery based on 0300 UTC, Vortex (Murasu) over Coastal Andhra\nPradesh and adjoining West-central bay and neighbourhood centered near 15.7N / 80.2E.\nAssociated scattered to broken low and medium clouds with embedded intense to very intense\nconvection lay over west Bay of Bengal, Coastal Andhra Pradesh, East Rayalaseema, South",
